WEBVTT

00:00:00.633 --> 00:00:12.601
Hand a ticket to Claude, Anthropic’s AI, and it does the work. Claude reads the ticket, works in your code, and Worklynq opens the pull request, with everything Claude did shown right on the ticket.

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:20.804
Connect GitHub first: Claude works in your code through it. Then, in Apps, under Developer tools, click Connect on Claude.

00:00:21.267 --> 00:00:31.080
Paste an Anthropic API key, from the Claude Console, under API keys. Claude’s usage is billed to that account by Anthropic, pay as you go.

00:00:32.067 --> 00:00:36.632
Once it’s connected, choose the Claude model to use, and the default base branch.

00:00:38.833 --> 00:00:47.089
Session limits protect your credits if a session gets stuck: a maximum run time, a stop when it’s idle, and a maximum cost per session.

00:00:47.533 --> 00:00:54.467
Then map each product to its repository. That’s where Claude works on that product’s tickets, always on its own branch.

00:00:55.467 --> 00:01:03.595
Here’s a bug on the ordering app: the tip isn’t added to group order totals. Its Claude section shows the repository Claude will work in.

00:01:04.033 --> 00:01:11.372
Click Hand this ticket to Claude, and add anything a new teammate would want to know. The ticket’s description always goes along.

00:01:11.833 --> 00:01:18.788
Click Start Claude. It reads the code, makes the change, and runs the tests, and you can follow along here, or stop it.

00:01:19.867 --> 00:01:28.123
When it’s done, Worklynq opens the pull request. The ticket shows Claude’s summary, the files it changed, what it did, and what it cost.

00:01:28.567 --> 00:01:36.375
Claude never merges its own work. Your team reviews the pull request, and Run Claude again starts a fresh session if it needs another go.

00:01:36.833 --> 00:01:49.015
You can also hand a ticket to Claude when you create it, or from a workflow, so every ticket that reaches a stage goes to Claude. Turn Claude on for each ticket type first, under Ticket Types, Sections.

